--- a/docs/avx-emulation.md
+++ b/docs/avx-emulation.md
@@ -1,39 +1,44 @@
 # AVX / SSE4.2 / POPCNT emulation (CPUs older than Sandy Bridge)
 
-The game code is built for the PS4's Jaguar CPU and uses AVX (128 and 256 bit), F16C, POPCNT and
-BMI1, thousands of times each. A host without them (Core 2, e.g. Q8400) raises an illegal
-instruction at each one. `src/avx_emulate.c` emulates them from the fault handler (`probe.c`):
-the instruction is decoded, executed, and the thread resumes after it.
+The game code is built for the PS4's Jaguar CPU and uses AVX (128 and 256 bit), F16C, POPCNT,
+BMI1, MOVBE and related instructions. A host without the required sets (for example, a Core 2
+Q8400) cannot execute those instructions natively. `src/avx_emulate.c` implements the software
+emulator; `scripts/isa_rewrite.py` identifies sites and the loader installs their trampolines.
 
-* On by default when the CPU lacks AVX, SSE4.2 or POPCNT. `BB_AVX_EMULATE=0` restores the old STOP.
-* Handled: the VEX forms (scalar and packed, 128 bit, and every 256 bit form that exists on Jaguar,
-  including the width-changing vcvt* and vmovmskps/vptest ymm), F16C, AES, PCLMUL, BMI1
-  (andn/bextr/blsi/blsr/blsmsk), vldmxcsr/vstmxcsr, vcmp predicates 0-31, and the SSE4.2 set:
-  CRC32, PCMPGTQ, PCMPESTRI/PCMPESTRM/PCMPISTRI/PCMPISTRM (legacy and VEX), plus POPCNT and MOVBE.
-* Still not covered: LZCNT/TZCNT (they run as BSR/BSF without a fault, so they cannot be trapped;
-  scripts/isa_rewrite.py rewrites them, which needs `pip install iced-x86`) and the 256 bit integer
-  AVX2 forms (vpaddd ymm...), which Jaguar does not have and eboot.bin does not use as code.
-  Every distinct AVX/POPCNT/SSE4.2 encoding found in eboot.bin is covered. An instruction that still
-  fails prints `AVX emulation stopped: <reason>: <bytes>`.
-* How: a VEX instruction that has a legacy SSE twin is rebuilt as that instruction and run natively
-  on fixed registers, so the host CPU computes the result; the ymm upper halves live in a per-thread
-  shadow. Instructions with no twin (vinsertf128, vpermilps, vbroadcast*, vmaskmov*, vtestps, F16C...)
-  are plain C.
-* Batching: one fault emulates the whole straight run of VEX instructions that follows (up to 128).
-  In eboot.bin the 573k AVX instructions sit in 153k runs (average 3.7, 35% of them in runs of 17+),
-  so this cuts the number of faults several-fold. Verified on random runs against real AVX hardware.
-* Cost: one exception dispatch per fault (microseconds). Expect a very low frame
-  rate; the point is to get the game running. Hot sites could later be rewritten to stubs
-  (the existing ISA rewrite mechanism) reusing the same core.
-* Windows caveat: the guest uses the System V red zone (128 bytes below rsp) and the Windows
-  exception dispatcher does not preserve it. An AVX instruction inside a leaf function with live
-  red-zone data corrupts that data. Linux (SIGILL) is unaffected.
+* Emulation is enabled by default when a fatal instruction set is missing. `BB_AVX_EMULATE=0`
+  restores the old STOP behavior.
+* On Windows, generic VEX instructions and the supported legacy POPCNT/CRC32/PCMPGTQ instructions
+  are rewritten before guest entry. The generated trampoline moves RSP down by 0x600 with LEA
+  (without touching memory), saves GPRs, XMM registers, flags and MXCSR below the guest red zone,
+  calls the emulator using the host ABI and restores the original state. It then executes any safe
+  displaced fall-through instructions and jumps back into the image. The original guest RSP and
+  its 128-byte SysV red zone are not used for the host call.
+* Windows does not use INT3 or VEH as a fallback for sites that need emulation. If a site cannot
+  be covered by a safe five-byte jump, the table marks it unsupported and the loader stops before
+  entering the game. If an unrewritten illegal instruction is nevertheless encountered, the VEH
+  reports it and refuses to continue. `BB_AVX_VEH_UNSAFE=1` explicitly re-enables the old unsafe
+  diagnostic path; do not use it for normal play.
+* Linux retains SIGILL emulation as a fallback. Static VEX and supported legacy sites are also
+  eligible for the load-time trampoline rewrite.
+* The scanner groups straight VEX runs up to the table's 15-byte instruction payload. It mirrors
+  the opcode whitelist in `src/avx_emulate.c`; unsupported opcode maps are marked unsupported
+  before execution. The runtime bridge verifies that the emulator consumed the complete copied
+  run, and aborts rather than silently skipping an unsupported instruction.
+* The emulator handles the VEX forms implemented in `src/avx_emulate.c`, including 128/256-bit
+  operations, F16C, AES, PCLMUL, BMI1 VEX forms, vldmxcsr/vstmxcsr, and the supported compare
+  predicates. The legacy path covers POPCNT, CRC32 and PCMPGTQ. MOVBE, BMI1 and LZCNT/TZCNT are
+  handled by the existing ISA rewrite/emulation path.
+* AVX upper halves (YMM bits 255:128) live in per-thread shadow state because the host CPU may not
+  have YMM registers. The trampoline preserves all 16 XMM registers, GPRs, RFLAGS and MXCSR around
+  the C bridge.
 
-## Test (needs a CPU with AVX, F16C, AES, PCLMUL, BMI1; Linux)
+## Trampoline validation
 
-    python3 scripts/avx_samples.py eboot.bin samples.txt
-    gcc -std=gnu11 -O1 -mno-red-zone tests/test_avx_emulate.c -o out/avx-test
-    out/avx-test samples.txt 3
+On Linux, `tests/test_avx_trampoline.c` executes a generated trampoline by jumping into it (not
+calling it), paints all 128 bytes below the guest RSP, and checks that the bridge sees the original
+RSP and intact red-zone bytes. It also checks the generated jump returns through the original
+return address. The test is included in the Linux `build.sh --test` path.
 
-Every encoding runs natively and through the emulator on the same random registers and memory;
-registers, memory and flags are compared.
+The full Windows build and a real Q8400 game run still need to be performed on a Windows machine.
+The trampoline's Win64 argument setup is emitted separately from the SysV test path; this local
+Linux validation does not substitute for a Windows ABI/build test.
